The {{Nihongo|'''Yuzawa Incident'''|湯沢の変|Yuzawa no Hen}} was a pivotal political crisis and abduction event that occurred on October 12, 1558, in the Mino Province of [[Okaiken]] (then known as Mitoyagi). Agents of the [[Ogasawara clan]] infiltrated the Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace and successfully abducted the two-year-old Imperial Prince Fumihito, who would later return to history as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] (Honda Shigetada).

The incident is historically significant as it marked the nadir of Imperial authority during the [[Sengoku period]], demonstrating the ability of regional ''daimyō'' to physically coerce the Imperial Court. While the Ogasawara clan successfully extorted massive territorial concessions from the Emperor, their eventual abandonment of the prince set in motion the chain of events that led to the rise of the [[Honda clan]] and the establishment of the [[Dual Sovereignty]] system.

==Background==
By the mid-16th century, the authority of the Imperial Family in [[Okaiken]] had eroded significantly. While they remained the spiritual figureheads of the nation and the signatories for the ''shōen'' (estates), actual military and economic power had fractured among warring feudal lords. The Imperial Court, residing in the capital of [[Okami]], possessed little ability to enforce its will beyond the palace walls.

The [[Ogasawara clan]], based in [[Mino Province]], was one of the most aggressive rising powers of the era. Led by [[Ogasawara Terumune]], the clan sought to legitimize their territorial expansion by obtaining official court titles and forcing the Imperial Family to appoint an Ogasawara heir as the ''Sei-i Taishōgun''. In the autumn of 1558, the Imperial family, including then-Crown Prince Yoshihito (later [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]), his wife Empress Sento-in, his brother Prince Mutsuhito, and his infant son Fumihito, traveled to the Mikasawa Summer Palace. Located within the nominal territory of the Ogasawara, the palace was poorly fortified, relying on the social taboo against attacking the Emperor for protection.

==The Incident==
On the night of October 12, 1558, a group ostensibly under the orders of Ogasawara Terumune breached the perimeter of the Mikasawa Palace. Historical accounts suggest the attackers faced little organized resistance due to the light garrison of the summer retreat.

During the chaos of the infiltration, Prince Mutsuhito, the uncle of the young prince, attempted to intercept the kidnappers and was assassinated. Crown Prince Yoshihito survived the raid uninjured but was unable to prevent the assailants from seizing his son. The two-year-old Prince Fumihito was removed from the palace grounds and transported to the Ogasawara clan's stronghold. The operation was designed not as an assassination attempt on the current line, but as a hostage extraction to gain leverage over the court.

==Negotiations and Concessions==
Following the abduction, the Ogasawara clan issued a series of exorbitant demands to the Imperial Court in exchange for the safety of the prince. Initially, Crown Prince Yoshihito refused to capitulate, upholding the dignity of the throne. However, the negotiations reached a breaking point when Ogasawara Terunobu threatened to execute the child regardless of his divine lineage.

Forced into submission, the Imperial Court agreed to the Ogasawara terms. The concessions fundamentally altered the balance of power in central Mitoyagi. The Ogasawara clan was granted legal lordship over the northern quarter of the island of [[Harujima]], a strategic territory that solidified their control over key trade routes. Additionally, the court transferred ''shōen'' rights amounting to 300,000 ''koku'' to Ogasawara retainers, bringing the clan's total estimated wealth to a staggering 1.5 million ''koku''. Politically, Ogasawara Terunobu was appointed as a ''Bugyō'' (Magistrate) of the Imperial Court, giving a warlord direct administrative authority over Imperial affairs for the first time in centuries.

==The Betrayal and Abandonment==
Despite the Imperial Court fulfilling all demands, the Ogasawara clan did not return Prince Fumihito. In a act of treachery that would later be cited as justification for the Ogasawara's destruction, the clan falsely reported to the Emperor that the young prince had contracted a sudden illness and died during the journey back to the capital.

In reality, the prince had become a liability. With hostilities intensifying across Mitoyagi as other clans reacted to the Ogasawara's sudden rise in status, the clan decided against keeping a high-profile Imperial hostage. Instead of returning him or killing him, they abandoned the child at the Shōhei Temple, a remote Buddhist monastery in Mino.

==Aftermath==
The ramifications of the Yuzawa Incident were far-reaching. Believing his son to be dead, Crown Prince Yoshihito (later Emperor Komei) issued secret decrees to rival clans, asking them to liberate the capital from Ogasawara influence, sparking decades of intensified warfare.

Unbeknownst to the court, the prince survived. Under the care of the monk Zen no Kishō, he was raised as a commoner acolyte named "Ichimatsu." He was converted to [[Jōdo-shū]] Buddhism and trained in the temple's daily labors. This upbringing, far removed from the sheltered life of the court, hardened the prince and allowed him to develop the martial skills and common touch that would later define his rule as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]].

The Ogasawara clan's deception held for nearly thirty years. It was not until the rise of the [[Honda clan]]—specifically after Honda Shigetatsu adopted Ichimatsu—that the prince began his ascendancy. The truth of his survival was only fully revealed to the Imperial Court in 1601, following the [[Battle of Tone]], when the conquering general Honda Shigetada was identified as the lost Prince Fumihito, bringing the tragedy of the Yuzawa Incident to a close with the restoration of Imperial power.

